UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 40, Lesson 1: Year 3 PDHPE reflection', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food, friendships, games, safety, gymnastics and dance, and healthy habits. Choose evidence such as scores, charts, plans and earlier reflections. Name strengths, challenges and next goals using specific examples. Everyone''s journey is different, and progress is measured against your own starting point.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose one unit and write a strength, a challenge and a next goal using one piece of evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play. Challenge: remembering to use them every time. Goal: practise at each crossing this month.'' It names evidence and a goal.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking reflection means saying everything went well. Honest reflection includes challenges and goals.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose four pieces of PDHPE work from different units. For each, write the skill it shows, one strength and one next goal. Pick your proudest piece and give a reason. Share only what you are comfortable sharing.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food, friendships, games, safety, gymnastics and dance, and healthy habits. Choose evidence such as scores, charts, plans and earlier reflections. Name strengths, challenges and next goals using specific examples. Everyone''s journey is different, and progress is measured against your own starting point.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-SMI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose four pieces of PDHPE work from different units. For each, write the skill it shows, one strength and one next goal. Pick your proudest piece and give a reason. Share only what you are comfortable sharing. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using reflection on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking reflection means saying everything went well.. Correction: Sample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking reflection means saying everything went well. Honest reflection includes challenges and goals. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating reflection on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with reflection, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about reflection with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for reflection. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for reflection. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Sample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play","Thinking reflection means saying everything went well"],"question":"Choose one unit and write a strength, a challenge and a next goal using one piece of evidence. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for reflection; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking reflection means saying everything went well","Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food","Sample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play","Careful use of a strength and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with reflection?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a strength and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply reflection to new material and name the a strength and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking reflection means saying everything went well","Name a strength and explain why it matters only, without linking it to reflection"],"question":"Which response best applies reflection with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on reflection. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking reflection means saying everything went well","Sample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for reflection?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking reflection means saying everything went well","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with reflection","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about reflection?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: Zoe''s reflection","Thinking reflection means saying everything went well"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about reflection?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for reflection?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking reflection means saying everything went well","Name a strength and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Choose four pieces of PDHPE work from different units"],"question":"Which independent response best applies reflection to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about reflection with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating reflection on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using reflection on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for reflection. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with reflection, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking reflection means saying everything went well.. Correction: Sample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w40-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise reflection.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use reflection with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a strength and explain why it matters.","I can practise a challenge independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a next goal needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food, friendships, games, safety, gymnastics and dance, and healthy habits.","options":["Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about reflection with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Zoe''s reflection. ''My strength was balance: I improved from 3 to 5 seconds (Week 31). Something I learned about friendships: using I statements helped solve a disagreement (Week 19). A challenge: remembering to drink water. My next goal: drink six cups on school days.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose one unit and write a strength, a challenge and a next goal using one piece of evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play. Challenge: remembering to use them every time. Goal: practise at each crossing this month.'' It names evidence and a goal.","explain":"Reflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food, friendships, games, safety, gymnastics and dance, and healthy habits. Choose evidence such as scores, charts, plans and earlier reflections. Name strengths, challenges and next goals using specific examples. Everyone''s journey is different, and progress is measured against your own starting point.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking reflection means saying everything went well. Honest reflection includes challenges and goals. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A strength and a challenge.","caption":"Use evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Strength</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">balance, 3 up to 5 seconds</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Challenge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">remember water</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Zoe''s reflection. ''My strength was balance: I improved from 3 to 5 seconds (Week 31). Something I learned about Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Zoe''s reflection. ''My strength was balance: I improved from 3 to 5 seconds (Week 31). Something I learned about Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about reflection with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Zoe''s reflection. ''My strength was balance: I improved from 3 to 5 seconds (Week 31). Something I learned about friendships: using I statements helped solve a disagreement (Week 19). A challenge: remembering to drink water. My next goal: drink six cups on school days.'' Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"A next goal.","caption":"Set a clear goal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Goal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">six cups on school days</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Proud</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one piece and a reason</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about reflection with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking reflection means saying everything went well. Honest reflection includes challenges and goals. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Zoe''s reflection. ''My strength was balance: I improved from 3 to 5 seconds (Week 31). Something I learned about friendships: using I statements helped solve a disagreement (Week 19). A challenge: remembering to drink water. My next goal: drink six cups on school days.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''Strength: I used the stop, look, listen, think steps in my Week 26 role play. Challenge: remembering to use them every time. Goal: practise at each crossing this month.'' It names evidence and a goal.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking reflection means saying everything went well. Honest reflection includes challenges and goals. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about reflection with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReflecting on the year means looking back at what you learned in feelings and wellbeing, movement skills, healthy food, friendships, games, safety, gymnastics and dance, and healthy habits. Choose evidence such as scores, charts, plans and earlier reflections. Name strengths, challenges and next goals using specific examples. Everyone''s journey is different, and progress is measured against your own starting point. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 40, Lesson 1: Year 3 PDHPE reflection","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":40,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Zoe''s reflection. ''My strength was balance: I improved from 3 to 5 seconds (Week 31). Something I learned about friendships: using I statements helped solve a disagreement (Week 19). A challenge: remembering to drink water. My next goal: drink six cups on school days.''\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A strength and a challenge.","caption":"Use evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Strength</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">balance, 3 up to 5 seconds</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Challenge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">remember water</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A next goal.","caption":"Set a clear goal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Goal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">six cups on school days</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Proud</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one piece and a reason</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w40-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
